Purpose and prior work
Finite-field modal quantum theory retains linear transformations and a distinction between possible
and impossible outcomes without adopting complex amplitudes or Born probabilities [6, 7]. This
makes the choice of oracle and readout decisive: an algebraic encoding of a truth table is not
automatically an operational algorithm for extracting its properties.
We give self-contained exact-query proofs for characteristic-two linear modal computation with
finite complete recorded instruments. For arbitrary fixed invertible pointwise oracle actions, one
query cannot solve Deutsch or Deutsch–Jozsa, and n queries are necessary to recover an n-bit
Bernstein–Vazirani secret. Standard XOR queries attain the Deutsch and Bernstein–Vazirani bounds.
We make the adaptive branch induction explicit and show why ring-valued kickback does not supply
an invertible Fourier analyzer. These statements refine known modal obstructions under explicit
access and readout contracts; historical firstness is not claimed.
James, Ortiz and Sabry already identify the failure of Deutsch’s usual advantage in their
conclusion [5]. We therefore do not present its qualitative failure as a new observation. Their
reversible finite-field language is also a foundation for distinguishing linear amplitude evolution
from arbitrary reversible Boolean computation on basis labels. The precise finite adaptive query
theorems below are the narrower subject of this note.
The method of bounding query algorithms by polynomial degree is established [1]. Exact
modal discrimination of independent states is likewise background [3]. Our argument combines
these ingredients with the fact that a Boolean parity control is a degree-one scalar polynomial in
characteristic two. Uniform complexity classifications over rings [2] concern a different question
